e problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSimplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2af3602d76c3376f3ee7bcf9a054d2c1d4481e4955dede0878b3c472bbe40c36"},"seconds":69.51863312721252,"seed":986795950601667472,"stop":"eos","text":"To simplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$, we can use trigonometric identities and properties to find a more straightforward form of the expression.\n\nStep 1: Use the product-to-sum identities or other trigonometric identities to simplify the expression. We start by using the identity for the product of cosines:\n\\[\n\\cos x \\cos y = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (x-y) + \\cos (x+y)].\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Pair the cosines in a way that simplifies the expression. We can pair $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$ and $\\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (10^\\circ - 70^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ + 70^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (-60^\\circ) + \\cos 80^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos 60^\\circ + \\cos 80^\\circ].\n\\]\nSince $\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} + \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Similarly, for $\\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (30^\\circ - 50^\\circ) + \\cos (30^\\circ + 50^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (-20^\\circ) + \\cos 80^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 80^\\circ].\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Combine the results from Steps 2 and 3:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\left( \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right).\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Use the product-to-sum identities again for $\\cos 20^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 20^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (20^\\circ - 80^\\circ) + \\cos (20^\\circ + 80^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos (-60^\\circ) + \\cos 100^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos 60^\\circ + \\cos 100^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\frac{1}{2} + \\cos 100^\\circ \\right).\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Simplify further using the identity $\\cos 100^\\circ = -\\cos 80^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 20^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Now substitute back into the expression:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right).\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Simplify the expression using the known trigonometric values and identities:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{16}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
